With \(V_{in}\) set to ac ground (as you do when finding \(Z_{out}\)), the gate of \(M_1\) is **not** a solid ac ground: it is grounded only through \(R_S\). Then any small‑signal variation at \(V_{out}\) (the **source** of \(M_1\)) couples back to the gate through \(M_1\)’s \(C_{gs}\). Because that coupled current must flow through \(R_S\), the gate voltage becomes a **frequency‑dependent, phase‑shifted** version of \(v_{out}\). The transconductance \(g_m\) then converts this phase‑shifted \(v_{gs}\) into a source/drain current, creating a reactive component in the incremental i–v relation at the output.

You can see the “inductor” explicitly with a simple small‑signal approximation (ignore \(r_o\) and other caps for the point being made):

Gate node (no external drive, only \(R_S\) to ground and \(C_{gs}\) to \(v_{out}\)):
\[
\frac{v_g}{R_S} + sC_{gs}(v_g-v_{out})=0
\;\Rightarrow\;
v_{gs}=v_g-v_{out}=-\frac{1}{1+sR_SC_{gs}}\,v_{out}
\]

The output current drawn by \(M_1\) is approximately \(i_{out}\approx g_m(v_{out}-v_g)=g_m\frac{1}{1+sR_SC_{gs}}v_{out}\), giving
\[
Z_{out}(s)=\frac{v_{out}}{i_{out}}
\approx \frac{1+sR_SC_{gs}}{g_m}
= \frac{1}{g_m} \;+\; s\underbrace{\frac{R_SC_{gs}}{g_m}}_{L_{eq}}
\]

That is a **resistance in series with an inductance** \(L_{eq}=R_SC_{gs}/g_m\). Hence \(Z_{out}\) has a **positive imaginary part** (\(+j\omega L\)) over the frequency range where this approximation holds (before other device capacitances and \(r_o\) dominate). \(M_2\) mainly adds parallel \(r_o\) and capacitance to ground; it doesn’t remove the inductive term created by the \(R_S\)–\(C_{gs}\)–\(g_m\) feedback in \(M_1\).
